WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.834
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.035
We're here with Mike Birbiglia.

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:04.537
And not to give too much away
from the show,

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.138
but in the title,
a pool is referenced.

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:07.707
And you started
going to the YMCA

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.042
to sort of help yourself
get healthier.

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.311
-Yeah, and it was funny,
'cause a few years ago,

00:00:12.311 --> 00:00:14.247
I was workshopping the show
in New York,

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:18.718
and a lot of the administration
of the YMCA

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.753
started coming to the show.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:24.757
And I talk about how there's
a lot of urine in the pool.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:29.462
And, like, that's, like,
the main thing I talk about

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:30.796
with the YMCA.

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:33.299
Like, I say, like --
Literally, I say, like,

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.068
there was a study
where these scientists

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:38.004
studied a 200,000-gallon pool

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:42.575
and concluded that there was
20 gallons of pure urine in it.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.979
And I said, it's too much.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.847
Percentage-wise,
it's not that much,

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:50.450
but if you picture it,
it's a full tank of gas.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.786
Like, that's a Ford F-150
full of urine.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:55.922
That'll get you to Pittsburgh.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:01:00.260
And the YMCA folks
came to the show,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.428
loved the show, came back again.

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:06.165
They're probably the most
consistent repeat customers

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:07.933
of the show,
which goes to show you --

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.602
and I'm sure you've experienced
this with this show

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.037
and with "SNL" over the years --

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.871
people like being made fun of.

00:01:12.871 --> 00:01:14.907
-Yeah, well, they just like
hearing their own name.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.176
I think they're just like,
"This is great."

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:19.378
You -- Like a lot of comedians,

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:21.314
'cause you've been workshopping
this show for years.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.615
During the pandemic,
a lot of avenues weren't open,

00:01:23.615 --> 00:01:25.318
obviously, indoor spaces.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:26.953
What were some
of the outdoor spaces

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.887
you were lucky enough
to perform at?

00:01:28.887 --> 00:01:36.195
-I was in all kinds of yards
and hotel parking lots.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:39.298
At one point
there was a train --

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:42.068
At one point there was
a racetrack in Jersey

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.870
where I think Mulaney and I
were doing a show one night --

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:46.806
John Mulaney.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.408
And it was actually the --

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.176
My agent, Mike Berkowitz,
was there,

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:51.911
and he watched my hour,

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.914
and he was like, "I think this
should be a Broadway show."

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.083
Which doesn't happen
at the racetrack that often.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.350
-Yeah.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.019
-But the reason why
was because --

00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:04.590
Because of the pandemic,
everyone was inside for so long

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.727
that a lot of the people
at these shows

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:11.497
weren't even fans of me and John
or even comedy.

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:12.665
They were just like,

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.767
"Something's happening
down the road!"

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:15.935
You know what I mean?

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.570
And they just showed up.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.438
And it was, like,
kids who were like 10,

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:20.707
people who were like 90,

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.542
and they all really liked
this material.

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.277
And that's why my agent, Mike,
was like,

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.879
"I think this could appeal to,
like, a wide swath of people."

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.380
And you even saw it last night.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.783
Our friend Mike brought
his kids, who are 12 and 14.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:31.984
They seemed to like the show.

00:02:31.984 --> 00:02:33.052
-It was fun --

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.253
You know, over the course
of the night,

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.288
I really did enjoy
looking around.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:38.457
And I do think this is
a very special week, right?

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.092
A lot of families are together.
-Yeah.

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:43.362
-And so this was a thing
they were confident

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:44.897
that all ages would enjoy,

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.433
and it seems, like, trite to be
like, "It's fun for all ages."

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.701
But it really was,

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.103
and I think that was
a cool thing about the show.

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:52.105
-Funny thing -- Like, last week,

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.974
there was a kid
in, like, the third row.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:56.475
And I go, like,
"Hey, how old are you?"

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:59.245
Sometimes if someone's really
young, I like to call it out,

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:02.248
like, "This might not
be appropriate for you."

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.817
Like, my last show, I might --

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.220
The new one on Netflix,
actually in the taping,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.688
there's, like, a young kid.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:12.392
Like, "I just want you to know
that I'm not inappropriate.

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.893
You just have bad parents."

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.227
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:21.634
-And so last week,
there was a kid who was --

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.670
seemed really young,
like, in the third row.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.870
And I go, "How old are you?"

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.939
And he goes
[High-pitched voice] "13!"

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:30.176
And I was just like,
"I don't think so."

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:33.479
And then I was like,
am I, like, carding this kid?

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.982
Like, he thinks I'm the bouncer
at the comedy show,

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.516
like, "Get out of here!

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:40.319
You're 7 years old!
Get out of here!"

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:41.587
But, yeah, no, it is.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:43.923
It's not fun for all ages,
but truthfully, like,

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:47.093
I think 12 and up is, like --
it's fun 'cause it's like --

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:50.396
It ends up -- Don't you think
it's a conversation starter?

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:51.897
-Yeah.
I think it really is.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:53.899
And I also -- I think --

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:56.001
We're about to embark
on our time of Thanksgiving,

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:57.837
which everybody could use,
I think,

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.671
a few conversation starters
at Thanksgiving

00:03:59.671 --> 00:04:01.173
and maybe some advice.

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:03.643
And you do some have very
helpful advice for Thanksgiving.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:04.877
-Here's my advice.

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:07.746
I was thinking about
relationships, marriage,

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.015
and how --

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.517
I think this can apply
to Thanksgiving, which is,

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.753
I think in a marriage,
and you --

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:17.457
I've been married 14 years,
and you've been about the same?

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:18.590
-Nine.

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:21.961
-Never mind.
-Yeah.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.130
-We've both been married
about a decade.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:24.964
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:28.500
-And I found that in a marriage,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.872
you got to always remember that
you can't predict the weather.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:35.108
You never know
how it's going to go.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:38.577
Always bring a raincoat
and an umbrella,

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:42.382
'cause otherwise, you're just
going to be like, "I'm soaked!

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.584
Why am I soaked," you know?

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:47.420
And that's how I feel
about Thanksgiving.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.721
You're going to be
with your family.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.489
And some of them
you're not going to like.

00:04:50.489 --> 00:04:52.691
So bring an umbrella.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:54.493
An emotional umbrella.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:56.696
-Not a physical one
to just, like, stick them with.

00:04:56.696 --> 00:04:57.763
Like, an emotional one.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:04:58.964
-That's my advice
for Thanksgiving.

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:00.433
-That's very good advice.

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.201
Other advice that I would give
everybody is go see the show.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.235
It's wonderful.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:04.270
It's always so nice
to have you here.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.237
Thank you so much.
-It's the best.

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:06.972
Thank you so much.
-Mike Birbiglia.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:08.808
"The Old Man and the Pool"

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.843
is playing at
the Vivian Beaumont Theater

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:13.279
an additional two-week extension
of its run.

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.048
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

